  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "crude",
      "title": "Crude tape",
      "blurb": "The price of the barrel. WTI vs Brent vs the paper ETFs. Curve (M1\u2013M2) tells you tightness: positive = backwardation (prompt demand / draw). WCS\u2013WTI is what Canadian heavy actually realizes. USDCAD is the FX overlay on CAD-cost / USD-WTI producers."
    },
    {
      "id": "products",
      "title": "Refined products",
      "blurb": "Gasoline (RBOB) and diesel/heating oil (ULSD) are what refiners actually sell. Quote is \u00a2/gal; \u00d70.42 converts to $/bbl. Soybean oil is the renewable-diesel / SAF feedstock."
    },
    {
      "id": "cracks",
      "title": "Crack spreads",
      "blurb": "Refining margin: product minus crude, in $/bbl. 3-2-1 = (2\u00d7 gasoline + 1\u00d7 distillate \u2212 3\u00d7 crude) / 3. Diesel cracks are the 2026 story; gasoline is the lagging leg. USGC is Calumet/Shreveport; NYH is the coastal print."
    },
    {
      "id": "bio",
      "title": "Biofuels \u00b7 RINs \u00b7 SOHO",
      "blurb": "The other Calumet P&L. D4 (biomass-based diesel) and D6 (ethanol) RINs are the EPA credit. SOHO = soybean oil minus heating oil \u2014 lower is better for renewable diesel/SAF (cheap feedstock vs diesel)."
    },
    {
      "id": "spr",
      "title": "SPR & government stocks",
      "blurb": "Strategic / government-controlled crude. US SPR is weekly EIA. IEA government-controlled stocks split Americas (mostly US SPR), Europe, and Pacific (Japan/Korea/Australia). Official China SPR is not published; port inventories are the observable proxy."
    },
    {
      "id": "inv",
      "title": "Commercial inventories",
      "blurb": "The weekly EIA print (Thursday 11:00 ET). Combined crude draw = commercial crude \u0394 + SPR 5-day net \u2014 that is the number Gavin throws at the glut narrative. API weekly is not on this Bloomberg entitlement; EIA is the official print. Cushing caps the WTI prompt."
    },
    {
      "id": "supply",
      "title": "Supply",
      "blurb": "US crude production (EIA). The ceiling on how fast the US can respond to a price spike."
    },
    {
      "id": "gas",
      "title": "Gas & LNG",
      "blurb": "Henry Hub is US gas. TTF is Europe. JKM is NE Asia LNG. US storage is the EIA weekly. Oil and gas can decouple; LNG links them when Europe/Asia are short."
    },
    {
      "id": "freight",
      "title": "Freight",
      "blurb": "Dirty tankers (crude) vs clean (products). BDTI ripping = barrels actually moving, or war-risk / longer-haul (Cape vs Hormuz). A freight spike with flat crude is a logistics story, not a demand story."
    },
    {
      "id": "hormuz",
      "title": "Hormuz transits",
      "blurb": "Bloomberg tanker-crossing counts through the Strait of Hormuz. Crude-tanker 7-day total is the mb/d proxy (pre-war this printed tens of crossings; ~1 is effectively shut). Daily crude barrels at 0 is the same tape. LNG and all-tanker 7d are the leak-around. Not AIS-perfect \u2014 still the series he wishes someone published as a hard mb/d."
    },
    {
      "id": "positioning",
      "title": "Positioning",
      "blurb": "CFTC crowding, not a retail-broker widget. Gavin\u2019s \u201871% of retail still short\u2019 is IG/OANDA-style; that feed is not on this Bloomberg. Spec (non-commercial) and managed-money net/shorts are the institutional equivalent. Spec short % = shorts / (longs+shorts)."
    },
    {
      "id": "equities",
      "title": "Equities & ETFs",
      "blurb": "How the tape is pricing it. CLMT is the specialty refiner/SAF name. VLO/MPC/PSX/DINO/PBF are the crack-spread beta. XOM/CVX/COP are the integrateds. XLE/XOP/OIH are the sector proxies. BNO tracks Brent; USO tracks WTI."
    }
  ],
